<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.p9.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="64.48" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 687.5 64.48" width="687.5"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,64.48) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000080;" fill="#000080" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.51 L 0 58.97 C 0 62.02 2.47 64.48 5.51 64.48 L 681.99 64.48 C 685.03 64.48 687.5 62.02 687.5 58.97 L 687.5 5.51 C 687.5 2.47 685.03 0 681.99 0 L 5.51 0 C 2.47 0 0 2.47 0 5.51 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2FF;" fill="#F2F2FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.57 5.51 L 1.57 58.97 C 1.57 61.15 3.34 62.91 5.51 62.91 L 681.99 62.91 C 684.16 62.91 685.93 61.15 685.93 58.97 L 685.93 5.51 C 685.93 3.34 684.16 1.57 681.99 1.57 L 5.51 1.57 C 3.34 1.57 1.57 3.34 1.57 5.51 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 13.39 12.14)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:47.75em;--ltx-fo-height:3.1em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="45.59" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 42.9)" width="660.73"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.p9.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:47.75em;">
<span id="S4.p9.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p9.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">✔But why should a few extra words make such a difference?<span id="S4.p9.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Because in multimodal learning, every additional semantic pattern becomes a bridge
that can tie abstract text to vision and more bridges mean stronger cross-modal alignment.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T5" class="ltx_table">
